 "article": {
  "title": "Vertical Farms: Growing Up, Not Out",
  "topic": "科技·农业",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 322,
  "paras": [
   "By 2050, the world will need to feed nearly ten billion people, yet the land available for farming is shrinking every year. Cities keep spreading, soil is being worn out, and climate change makes harvests less certain. For a growing number of engineers and entrepreneurs, the answer is not to find more land, but to stop needing it: grow food indoors, in tall stacks of trays, close to the people who will eat it.",
   "These \"vertical farms\" look more like laboratories than fields. Crops are planted not in soil but in nutrient-rich water, a method known as hydroponics, and stacked in layers that reach several metres high. Special LED lamps replace sunlight, and computers control temperature, water and minerals around the clock. Because the environment is fully controlled, a single room can produce the same amount as many hectares of open field, and it can do so all year round, whatever the weather outside.",
   "The benefits reach beyond space. A vertical farm uses up to 95 percent less water than a traditional one, since the water is collected and reused instead of soaking away. Pests are rare indoors, so farmers can drop most pesticides. And because the food is grown inside the city, it travels only a few kilometres to the table, cutting transport emissions and keeping vegetables fresh. Restaurants and supermarkets in Tokyo, Singapore and Berlin already buy salad leaves picked the same morning.",
   "Yet the idea is not a cure-all. Lighting and cooling use a great deal of electricity, so a vertical farm is only as green as the power that feeds it. Only a handful of crops, mostly leafy greens, herbs and berries, grow well this way, and building the towers costs far more than buying a field. Still, as cities grow and farmland shrinks, growing up rather than out may prove to be one of the simplest answers to an old question: how do we keep everyone fed?"
  ],
  "zh": "到本世纪中叶，全球要养活近一百亿人，而可耕地却逐年减少。于是越来越多的工程师和创业者把目光转向室内：用层层叠叠的托盘、营养液和 LED 灯种植，不依赖土地与天气，这就是「垂直农业」。它比传统农业省水、少用农药、运输距离更近；但它耗电大、成本高，且只适合叶菜、香草、浆果等少数作物，能否真正推广还要看电从哪来。作者认为，随着城市扩张、农田萎缩，「向上种」或许是回答「如何养活所有人」的一条简单思路。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "For a growing number of engineers and entrepreneurs, the answer is not to find more land, but to stop needing it: grow food indoors, in tall stacks of trays, close to the people who will eat it.",
    "cn": "对越来越多的工程师和创业者来说，答案不是去找更多的地，而是不再需要地：在室内、在层层叠叠的高架上种植，靠近将要食用它的人。",
    "why": "not...but... 并列结构，冒号后是对前句的具体说明；写作中可用「not to...but to...」表达对比性主张。"
